  "note": "Independent source rejudgment against RFC 4659 Section 3.2.1.1: \"The link-local address shall be included in the Next Hop field if and only if the advertising BGP speaker shares a common subnet with the peer the route is being advertised to\"; the same section defines the global as the advertising speaker's. Read all tagged tests, helpers, cited producers and current native configs/checker. Static, API, forwarding and next-hop-rewrite tests assert exact zero-RD 48-octet pairs on-link versus 24-octet fields off-link. The loopback-global cases distinguish this peer-only condition from RFC 2545's joint condition; configured, relay and next-hop-self rails retain the pair despite a speaker-owned global outside the connected prefix. linkLocalConditionFor selects the VPN peer-only condition and the builders preserve the two zero RDs. The functional positive asserts the 48-octet field. The moved checkVPNFRR positive now requires BIRD's exact ordered global plus topology-owned link-local; FRR's output cannot independently prove the pair. Its negative remains only a third-party-global omission control on the SAME on-link sessions, not evidence of no common subnet; the true no-common-subnet negative is provided by the Go units and is not credited to that interop tag. Source limitations for non-speaker-owned globals remain outside this section's defined global-address form; no new whole-RFC support claim follows. Verdict enforced on the stated requirement. No native run, proof replay or freshness verification performed.",
